Close the review's findings on the required-checks contract

The record drift first, because it is the finding with a claim in it. Both
documents said #47 blocked force pushes to `main`. It did not: the ruleset
has carried `non_fast_forward` since it was created on 2026-08-05, so the
gap #66 recorded on the way past was closed before anybody noticed it was
load-bearing. What this ticket adds is the assertion that fails if the rule
is ever removed, and ADR-0013 and `public/README.md` now say that instead.

The ADR's discharge claim is dated to the merge that puts the tenth job on
`main` rather than to the ruleset edit, and the window between the two is
named where the order is argued: until that merge a branch cut from `main`
carries nine of the ten jobs and would sit pending on the tenth. It closes
by merging, not by waiting.

The rest is legibility. A comment line that ran to 143 characters is
rewrapped into its own paragraph — the second keyless-and-not-offline job
was jammed into the tail of ADR-0008's sentence. `API`,
`REQUIRED_STATUS_CHECKS` and `NON_FAST_FORWARD` argue their own why, like
every other constant in the module. `fetch_branch_rules` loses two
parameters nothing passed: there is one ruleset this check is about, and a
parameter invites a caller to ask about a different one. The suite parses
the bytes it already holds instead of reading `ci.yml` twice. Two of the
three new ADR paragraphs name the ticket they belong to.

Two findings are answered in place rather than changed: `REPO` stays
re-derived rather than imported from `tests/fixtures.py`, because the job
that runs this file has to stand when the suites around it are cut, and the
synthetic payload keeps GitHub's own spellings, because a transcription
written through the reader's vocabulary agrees with the reader by
construction. Both now say so.

Ship line `#8` is walked and recorded on #47.

